# .pf-assumptions — the terms this audit marks ASSUMED, one anchored regex per line.
#
# Read by audit-framework/checks/check-prose.sh (P1): each phrase below is grepped across every
# chapter, and any mention NOT sitting next to a qualifier (ASSUMED / not witnessed / unexercised
# / specified from / cannot be / PROVISIONAL / …) is reported. That is the automated form of the
# sweep that found this repo's worst round-2 defect — findings.md asserting "genuine Fisher-Yates
# with modulo-bias rejection" as *proven* while three other chapters correctly called it ASSUMED.
# It survived a human sweep by two independent reviewers and was mechanical to catch.
#
# The rule this encodes: a term qualified ASSUMED anywhere must be qualified EVERYWHERE. A claim
# is only as strong as its weakest statement of itself, and a reader who lands on the unqualified
# sentence has been told something the evidence does not support.
#
# One phrase per line; lines starting with # are comments.

# ── Premise 1: the maxFair modulo-bias rejection branch ─────────────────────────────────
# Specified from the client bundle. The expected number of rejection events across the
# capture's 172,500 draws is 2.024e-4 and 0 occurred (measured — outputs/audit-figures.json
# -> moduloBiasRejectionCensus), so a no-rejection implementation reproduces all 6,900 layouts
# identically. A capture this size would witness the branch with probability ~0.02% — SMALL,
# NOT ZERO. "This capture did not" is a measurement; "no capture could" is not, and no mention
# may say the latter (QA-12, 2026-09-10).
# Simulation cannot witness it as OPERATOR behaviour either — Pass 1 runs the AUDITOR's
# implementation, which reproduces whatever the auditor wrote. It DOES exercise the branch in
# the auditor's code: nonce 760455 / cursor 12 / range 13 on the committed m=5 Pass-1 row
# rejects chunk 4294967288 and takes 412214211 -> 6 (deleting the branch gives 1). No mention
# may claim the 24,000,000 simulated rounds contained no rejection. Every mention must say so.
modulo-bias rejection
#
# THE PARAPHRASES. Added 2026-09-09 after a reviewer found FIVE sites stating the branch as
# proven operator behaviour — game-logic-verifiability, glossary (twice), system-architecture —
# every one of which evaded the check above by never using the tracked phrase. A rule that only
# catches the wording we happened to think of is a rule that catches nothing: the claim is what
# is tracked, not the spelling. These are the constructions that assert the branch:
maxFair
rejects any chunk
every tile is exactly equally likely
below the modulo-bias ceiling

# ── Premise 1b: the cursor + 1_000_000 retry path ───────────────────────────────────────
# The branch taken when no 4-byte chunk of a digest falls below maxFair. Same status as the
# rejection ceiling it belongs to: specified, never exercised.
cursor \+ 1_000_000

